+{"q": 149, "question": "Who is the most likely murderer?", "narrative": "In the quiet of a home office, Mackenzie met a fiery end via a flamethrower, leaving Detective Winston tasked with questioning the two unlikely suspects, Sophia and Virgil.\n\nAs Winston closed the victim's office door behind him, he hummed a tune from one of the old tunes he had heard growing up. A home office painted with scorch marks and scars from flames, not the ordinary scene he would walk into. Mackenzie's death was indeed beyond ordinary.\n\nA local artist, Sophia, was on Winston's list for interviews. The name Sophia resonated in the art world, known for her bizarre fire-based creations. Winston remembered; the art reviews had used words like \"charred\" to describe her artwork. Moreover, he knew that she was often seen transporting flamethrowers to her studio, intriguing the townsfolk. It was said that she used these flamethrowers in her creative process.\n\nWinston found Sophia in her studio, surrounded by antiques that greatly resemble family heirlooms. But he knew this couldn't be the case as both Sophia's mother and sibling had confirmed to him that they did not have possession of any family heirlooms. Therefore, Sophia must have been purchasing these artifacts, and his suspicions were confirmed when he noticed the receipt from a local antique dealer. It was the same dealer who had mentioned that Sophia paid a high price for each item.\n\n\"Sophia?\" Winston asked, catching the attention of the artist, who had her back to him.\n\n\"Yes, that's me,\" Sophia replied, pushing her disheveled hair behind her ear. Her face was thin and pale. Dark circles hung heavy under her eyes.\n\n\"You purchase a lot of antiques, Sophia?\" Winston had noticed the receipt and the dealer's confirmation of the high prices.\n\n\"Yes, I do,\" Sophia replied, casting a glance around her at the collected objects. \"They're all pretty expensive, but worth the uniqueness they bring to my art.\"\n\nWinston decided to press about a more sensitive issue. \"Mackenzie,\" he said, and Sophia's expression shifted. \"She found your stash, didn't she?\"\n\nSophia\u2019s face paled even further. \"No comment,\" she muttered, but her jittery hands gave her away.\n\n\"I understand she had been funding your art and supporting your addiction habit...your addiction, Sophia,\" Winston added the words slipping past his lips. He revealed how Mackenzie had threatened to cut Sophia off from her art funds unless she got clean and to tell the world about Sophia's addiction if she didn't get help.\n\nSophia looked away, a flame dancing in her eyes, mirroring the charred elements in her artwork. In those eyes, Winston found a desperate artist drowning in her own addiction and, the financial torment that was coming along with it. A need for money, for drugs.\n\n\"Sophia, her threats...you did hear them, didn't you?\" Winston's voice was low.\n\nSophia, barely audible, whispered, \"I heard her.\"\n\nThe evidence was there: the futile search for family heirlooms, the expensive antiques, the thinly-veiled threats from Mackenzie, all leading towards an artist struggling with her addiction. An artist desperate for money and lost in her need for drugs.\n\n\"Thank you, Sophia,\" Winston stood from his chair, but before he left, his eyes trained on a flamethrower stored in the corner of the studio. With these parting words, he left, \"Take care of yourself, Sophia.\"\n\nAs Detective Winston entered Virgil's home gym, the smell of iron and sweat filled his nostrils. Walls lined with mirrors reflected the image of a man who spent a great deal of time building his formidable strength and size through lifting heavy weights. \n\n\"Detective,\" Virgil called out in a deep, gravelly voice, his muscular arms moving a stacked barbell with almost mechanical precision. \"Couldn't help but notice Mackenzie in your stack of files.\"\n\n\"She mentioned you helped her out around her home office. Moving heavy things