+{"q": 172, "question": "Who is the most likely murderer?", "narrative": "In the middle of pli\u00e9s and pirouettes, a deadly crossbow bolt finds its mark, leaving Ryan lifeless in the dance studio and launching Detective Winston into a tense pas de deux with suspects Rex and Mandy.\n\nDetective Winston had barely finished his coffee when he received the call about the murder at the dance studio. Ryan, a notable figure and beloved dance instructor and advocate for racial integration, was found dead in the wee hours of the morning. The news from the officer stated that there was only one person present, Rex, at the time of the murder. \n\nCrossbow, a peculiar choice of weapon, but Rex was a known historical weapons enthusiast and was found alone in the studio. Rex was a man known in the community to frequently utter his disgust and hatred for other races. He never shied away from making his views known and didn't even care about the public backlash he received.\n\nWinston quickly drove to the scene. His previous encounters with Rex had taught him that he was not the type to embrace change. Rex was a man of his unpleasant prejudices and it's appalling how such hatred could run so deep.\n\nWalking into the studio's vast echoing space, Winston noticed Rex hunched over a droning gramophone, a scattering of musical notes scattered on the floor. As if revealing a questionable love affair, the air was robust with the scent of violin rosin and vintage tunes. It was evident that Rex had been working that night on his compositions.\n\nRex then stated, in his usual gruff manner, \"Found something?\" \n\n\"I guess hard work pays off in its own way,\" Rex replied, confirming his solitary presence during the late work hours coinciding with Ryan's death.\n\n\"What an extravagant collection!\" Winston commented, looking at the decorated wall filled with a series of historical crossbows that Rex had acquired from an auction. Winston remembered people mentioning how Rex often admired